<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Calibration of per-feature firing thresholds</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Co-occurrence evaluation with BatchTopK SAEs poses a subtlety: the top-<math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
selection is applied over an entire batch, so whether feature <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> fires on a
given token depends on what other tokens are in the batch.
This makes firing a batch-composition-dependent decision, which is unsuitable
for accumulating reproducible per-token co-occurrence statistics.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We resolve this by calibrating a per-feature firing threshold <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> using
an equal-error-rate (EER) criterion on 5M tokens.
For each feature <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we find <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the rate at which the feature
fires above <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (under <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>-thresholding) matches its BatchTopK firing
rate as closely as possible.
Co-occurrence statistics are then computed using these fixed thresholds:
feature <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> fires on token <math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if its pre-activation exceeds <math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
independently of other tokens.
This gives reproducible, batch-composition-independent binary firing indicators.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Firing rate distributions</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Per-feature firing rates for the joint (<math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>) and
solo SAEs were computed over 35M tokens using the calibrated EER thresholds.
Both distributions show closely matched spread and shape: mean firing rate
per feature is identical by construction (BatchTopK with L0 = 64 fixes the
aggregate), and the per-feature distributions are comparable.
No long tail of near-zero-activity features appears in the joint condition,
ruling out near-dead features as a driver of the <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\varphi|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\varphi|</annotation></semantics></math> reduction.
Firing rate data are stored in the <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">N1_joint</span> and <span id="A2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">N1_solo</span>
vectors of <span id="A2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cross_phi_topk.npz</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span><math id="A3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\varphi|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\varphi|</annotation></semantics></math> distribution shape</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Approximately 75.7% of all <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>209M feature pairs have <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>:
most pairs are weakly anti-correlated by construction, since sparse activations
in a large overcomplete dictionary cannot push many pairs simultaneously positive.
The median <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\varphi\approx-5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo rspace="0em">≈</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\approx-5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>; the mean is slightly positive due
to a heavy right tail reaching <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\approx 0.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\approx 0.13</annotation></semantics></math> for the most strongly
co-activating pairs.
Joint training acts specifically on the positive tail (Figure <a href="#S5.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 5.1 Feature co-occurrence ‣ 5 Results ‣ MetaSAEs: Joint Training with a Decomposability PenaltyProduces More Atomic Sparse Autoencoder Latents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>),
redistributing mass toward zero without meaningfully altering the dominant
anti-correlated region.
This is consistent with the subspace-blending interpretation: the penalty acts on
directions that share structure (positive <math id="A3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>), leaving uncorrelated or
anti-correlated pairs unchanged.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Activation norm context</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-2 large residual stream at layer 20: mean <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|_{2}=133.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>133.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|_{2}=133.3</annotation></semantics></math>,
std <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=224.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>224.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=224.6</annotation></semantics></math> (heavy-tailed; std <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> mean reflects occasional large-norm residuals).
Solo SAE L2 <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0.097" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.097</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0.097</annotation></semantics></math> is 0.073% of mean norm; the additional joint cost of
<math id="A4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.010" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.010</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.010</annotation></semantics></math> is 0.0075%.
This framing clarifies that the <math id="A4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+9.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>9.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+9.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> relative L2 increase, while
non-negligible as a fraction of the SAE’s own reconstruction error, is
essentially invisible relative to the magnitude of the underlying representations
being encoded.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Qualitative case-study tables</h2>

<figure id="A5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="A5.T2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">“too” polysemy split.</span> Solo feature 2827 conflates the
excess-degree sense (<span id="A5.T2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it was too hot</span>) with the sentence-final additive
sense (<span id="A5.T2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">me too</span>; <span id="A5.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">that’s true too</span>). The joint SAE separates
them: feat. 4741 inherits the excess sense (<math id="A5.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\varphi|=0.710" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.710</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\varphi|=0.710</annotation></semantics></math>), while
feat. 14213 captures the additive sense (<math id="A5.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\varphi|=0.799" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.799</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\varphi|=0.799</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
